  "worked_solution": "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto $xy$-plane.  \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2y+1,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
